          Yaden et al., 2018)
          (Gaggioli et al., 2019;
          Grossi et al., 2020;
          Riva et al., 2020)
Recent explosive growth in computational science and technology (CST) has accelerated the evolvement of PP. By enabling researchers to collect large-scale data from diverse sources such as social media and analyze them using machine learning methods, CST has facilitated an increased understanding of the complexity of wellbeing and the growing accessibility and flexibility of strategies that aid its enhancement. We propose the formalization and development of this emerging field as computational positive psychology (CPP), which integrates PP with principles, methods, and tools derived from CST to advance the understanding of wellbeing and foster evidence-based strategies for sustainable human flourishing in the digital age. CPP goes beyond merely applying computational models in PP for measuring wellbeing through the analysis of social media data. Meanwhile, it is diverges from positive technology and computing, a field that integrates the principles of PP into the human -computer interactive design to promote mental health and wellbeing. We conceive of CPP as a novel approach that foresters the advancement of PP theory, methodologies, and practices through a synergistic collaboration between PP and CST, and thereby contributes to the broader field of wellbeing science.

