Around 2010, the rise of social media platforms and big data catalyzed research into online language and behavioral data. Researchers employed computational methods such as machine learning (ML), natural language processing (NLP), social network analysis (SNA), to investigate wellbeing on a larger scale. This led to significant methodological advancements in PP. For example, Schwartz et al. used language from social media to forecast happiness levels, providing an unobtrusive measurement tool for wellbeing and other psychological variables. In addition to language analysis, machine learning has further empowered researchers to analyze emotions using diverse information sources. Multimodal emotion recognition technology can automatically evaluate and synthesize data collected from facial expressions, voice, gesture, eye movement, thermal imaging, and physiological signals, enabling continuous monitoring of an individual's wellbeing. In wellbeing research, the widespread use of wearable devices has also facilitated the efficient collection of physiological indicators, including heart rate and blood pressure, during individuals' daily routines (e.g..
